AK Badrul Huq J: By this application under Article 102 of the Constitution of the People&#39;&#39;s Republic of Bangladesh petitioner, Mr. Obaid Jaigirdar, Settler and Chairman of the Board of Trustees of OJ Trust called in question the Telex Message No.VIII-267/84 /9-J. 3(A) dated 29-1-1990 issued by the respondent No. 2, Deputy Secretary, Ministry of Land, Government of Bangladesh whereupon a Rule Nisi was issued calling upon the respondents to show cause as to why the action said to have been proposed to be taken against the petitioner vide Annexure-M to the petition should not be declared to have been made without lawful authority and is of no legal effect and further why they shall not be directed to cancel, rescind or withdraw from taking such action.&nbsp; 

 Petitioner by a registered deed of Trust dated 3-12-79 created a Trust for welfare and charitable purposes. The trustees of the Trust decided to undertake a Housing Estate Scheme for the purpose of utilizing the income therefrom for the welfare and charitable purposes of the Trust. In the month of January, 1980, the petitioner approached the then Minister-in-Charge of the Ministry of Land Administration and Land Reforms for allotment of Government Khas land for the said Trust. Petitioner on 16-1-80 submitted an application for settlement of Government khas land to the said Minister who on the body of the application passed on order for submission of enquiry report. Respondent No. 4, the Additional Deputy Commissioner (Revenue), Sylhet on 13-2-1980 submitted report. The Minister on consideration of the report passed an order for settlement of 31 acres of khas land of plot No. 1 of Mouza Debpur, PS Kotwali, Dist. Sylhet in favour of the OJ Trust at a salami of Taka 1,50,000.00. Respondent No. 4, Additional Deputy Commissioner (Revenue), in obedience to the said order of the Minister, allotted the said land in favour of OJ Trust, which paid the salami and Government Tahsilder delivered possession of the said land on 20-2-1980 to the Trust and, thereafter, on 22-2-1980 respondent No. 4, executed and registered lease deed in favour of OJ Trust.&nbsp; 

 3. It is the further case of the petitioner that the Superintendent of the Government Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm put forward claim on the said land and approached the Government for not allowing the said Trust to utilize the land. It transpired, subsequently, that 228 acres of land including the disputed land were acquired for Cattle Breeding- cum-Dairy Farm in the year 1932 in Land Acquisition Case Nos. 220-240 of 1930-31. The said land was not recorded in the name of the said Farm but recorded as Government khas land. The Ministry of Land Administration and Land Reforms requested the Ministry of Agriculture, Fisheries and Livestock&rsquo;s to give up the claim over the said land and the Ministry of Fishery and Livestock had been pleased to agree to surrender the said 21 acres of land in favour of OJ Trust and the said decision was communicated by a Memo dated 5th of January, 1986 (Annexure-&#39;&#39;H&#39;&#39;). Subsequently, the Ministry of Land Administration and Land Reforms by a Memo dated 21-5-86 (Annexure-T) cancelled the allotment granted in favour of OJ Trust and asked the Deputy Commissioner, Sylhet to revoke the deed of lease and find out khas land elsewhere for the Trust. The petitioner&#39;&#39;s prayer dated 31-5-86 addressed to the Government for revision of the said decision stood rejected and the petitioner was asked to submit proposal for lease of alternative Government khas land. Thereafter, the said Ministry revoked the earlier decision dated 21-5-86 and the Deputy Land Reforms Commissioner by a Memo dated 24-12-88 (Annexure-&#39;&#39;K&#39;&#39;) conveyed the decision of the Ministry to the Deputy Commissioner, Sylhet.&nbsp; 

 4. It is also the further case of the petitioner that the trustees of the Trust proceeded with the work of Housing Estate in full swing, built a mosque with the help of the local people and established a Bazar nearby and took all preparations for constructing Housing Estate. The respondent No. 2, all on a sudden, sent the impugned Telex Message dated 29-1-1990 (Annexure-M) to the Deputy Commissioner, Sylhet communicating the decision that the allotment of said land to OJ Trust be kept in abeyance. In the meantime respondent No. 3 filed frivolous Title Suit No. 18 of 1990 in the Second Court of Subordinate Judge, Sylhet against the petitioner and others for declaring the lease in favour of the petitioner as illegal, inoperative, motivated, collusive and without jurisdiction and in that suit plaintiffs prayer for temporary injunction has been opposed by the petitioner and the matter is pending for decision.&nbsp; 

 6. In answer to the Rule the respondent No. 1, Government of Bangladesh, represented by the Secretary, Ministry of Land entered appearance and filed affidavit-in-opposition. Thereafter the respondent No. 3, Secretary to the Ministry of Fisheries and Livestock also entered appearance and filed Affidavit-in-opposition. In both the Affidavits-in-oppositions the material allegations made in the writ petition have been stoutly denied.&nbsp; 

 7. In the Affidavit-in-opposition filed on behalf of the respondent No. 1, it is stated that the said 31 acres of land of plot No. 1 of Mouza Debpur, PS Kotwali, District, Sylhet was acquired in Land Acquisition Case No. 235 of 1930-31 for Government Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm, Sylhet and the said Farm has been in possession of the said land since 1932. The said land was recorded as khas land by mistake in the khas khatian. The Khatian illegally prepared in the name of OJ Trust with respect to the land in question was cancelled by the Revenue Authority on 6-1-87. It is further stated and asserted that the Ministry of Land had no interest in the land in question as the same was acquired for Government Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm. The Government in Memo No. V-239/84/559 dated 23-8-84 addressed to the Deputy Commissioner, Sylhet categorically stated that the land in question belonged to Government Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm, Sylhet and the land was wrongly leased out to OJ Trust and asked the Deputy Commissioner to do the needful.&nbsp; 

 8. In the Affidavit-in-opposition filed on behalf of the respondent No. 3, It is stated that 228 acres of land including the land in question was acquired in LA Case Nos. 220-240 of 1930-31 for Government Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm, Sylhet and since the year 1932, the land is under the possession of Government Castle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm under the Ministry of Fisheries and Livestock&rsquo;s bounded by RCC posts and barbad wire fencing. The Ministry of Livestock&rsquo;s and Fisheries is the rightful owner and possessor of the said land and the Ministry of Land Administration and Land Reforms got no legal authority to lease out the said land in favour of the petitioner and the leasing out of the land is absolutely illegal, without lawful authority and void a ab-initio. The Ministry of Land Administration and Land Reforms by the order dated 21-5-86 cancelled the allotment in favour of OJ Trust. The land in question having been in exclusive possession of the Sylhet Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm since 1932, there arose no occasion on the part of the petitioner to get into possession over the said land. It is further stated that in reply to the letter dated 16-1-85, this respondent informed the respondent No. 1 by the letter dated 27-1-85 that the business and functions of Government Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm has been increasing day by day and, as such, the question of giving up 31 acres of land from 228 acres of land of the Farm did not arise at all and the order, if any, was obtained from the Ministry of this respondent, that was done by some officers of the Ministry being gained over by the petitioner. It is the further case of respondent No. 3 as stated in the Affidavit-in-opposition that the order dated 24-12-88 got no force of law as because this respondent is the owner of the land in question since the year 1932 and the respondent No. 1 got no lawful authority to deal with the said land in any way.&nbsp; 

 10. Mr. Mahmudul Islam, learned Advocate appearing on behalf of the petitioner, challenged the legality of the impugned Telex Message Annexure-M, on the ground that the Government as the owner of the land in question having settled the same in favour of petitioner&#39;&#39;s OJ Trust by registered deed of lease and the petitioner having acquired indefeasible title to the land, the Government cannot unilaterally revoke the said settlement and the impugned Telex Message suffers from clear illegality.&nbsp; 

 12. Mr. KM Saifuddin Ahmed, learned Deputy Attorney-General appearing for both the respondent Nos. 1 and 3, on the other hand contended that the land in question along with other land had been acquired in the year 1931-32 for Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm and the said Farm is under the control of the Ministry of Fisheries and Livestock and that Ministry is the absolute owner and possessor of the said land and the Ministry of Land Administration and Land Reforms having got no legal authority to deal with the affairs of any other Ministry, got no authority to lease out any land owned and possessed by the Ministry of Fisheries and Livestock&rsquo;s and the lease in favour of the petitioner OJ Trust is absolutely illegal, without any lawful authority and same is void ab-initio. Learned Deputy Attorney- General while developing his argument further contended that the acquired 228 acres of land including the land in question is well bounded by RCC Pillars and barbed wire fencing and the same is in exclusive possession of the Sylhet Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm since 1932 and the possession as alleged by the petitioner is nothing but a pretended delivery of possession by persons not competent to give delivery of possession of the land in favour of OJ Trust and it was never actual delivery of possession and was a mere paper transaction.&nbsp; 

 16. The admitted position is that 228 acres of land had been acquired in LA Case Nos. 220-240 of 1930-31 including 31 acres of land in question for Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm at Sylhet. The said Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm is admittedly under the control and management of the respondent No. 3, Ministry of Fisheries and Livestock of the Government of Bangladesh. It is not denied by the petitioner that the said 228 acres of land including 31 acres of land in question had not been acquired for Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm at Sylhet. It is the positive common case set up by both the respondent Nos. 1 and 3 that Sylhet Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm had been and has been in possession of the entire acquired 228 acres of land including 31 acres of land in question since the year 1932. The respondent No. 1, the Ministry of Land Administration and Land Reforms in the affidavit-in-opposition categorically stated that the land in question belonged to Sylhet Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm which is under the control of the respondent No. 3&#39;&#39;s Ministry of Fisheries and Livestock. In the backdrop of this categorical assertion made by the respondent No. 1 as to the respondent No. 3&#39;&#39;s control over the land in question, the point became manifestly clear that it was only respondent No. 3&#39;&#39;s Ministry who had the exclusive jurisdiction and competence to lease out the land in possession of the Sylhet Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm. The respondent No. 1&#39;&#39;s Ministry did not have any authority, whatsoever, to lease out the disputed land in favour of the petitioner OJ Trust, merely because, they said land was not recorded in the name of the Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm but wrongly recorded in the khatian as Government khas land.&nbsp; 

 17. Perused the writ petition and the documents annexed thereto, the two affidavits-in-opposition filed by respondent Nos. 1 and 3 and the documents annexed thereto. We also perused the relevant Government files produced before us by the learned Deputy Attorney-General. On consideration of all these papers and documents we find that the process leading to the execution and registration of the lease deed had been done in a hot haste. OJ Trust was created on 3-12-79 by a registered deed of Trust with the sum of Taka one lac as its fund. Annexure-B is the deed of Trust. The Trust was created for the purpose of performing various charitable purposes, such as relief of the people in general and specially for the poor in regard to their education, health, medical treatment and for general cultural and social activities and other amenities of life and also for various research and scientific projects and religious and charitable purposes. The deed of Trust authorizes the trustees to invest the Trust Fund in any business, industry, security, etc. as the trustees may decide. In the month of January, 1980, petitioner met the Hon&#39;&#39;ble Minister-in-charge of the Ministry of Land Administration and Land Reforms for allotment of khas land in favour of the said Trust. An application had been filed before the said Minster on 16-1-80. Annexure-C is the said application. We perused the said application. In the application, nowhere it was stated, that the income of the Housing Estate would be utilized for the purposes of the Trust. The prayer for allocation of Government khas land at Sylhet was for a Housing Estate Scheme for the expatriation living in Britain. The Hon&#39;&#39;ble Minister on the body of the application passed an order in the following words: &quot;ADC (Rev.), Sylhet will please enquire and report positively within 2 weeks whether the lands are vacant khas lands and could be leased out to the petitioner trust on long term basis. To 7-2-80 for orders.&quot; Enquiry report was submitted on 13-2-80. The Hon&#39;&#39;ble Minister on 16-2-80 passed an order for settlement of the land in favour of petitioner OJ Trust at a salami of Taka 1,50,000.00. The possession of the allotted land was allegedly given to the petitioner on 20-2-80. Deed of lease was executed and registered on 22-2-80. Annexure-F is the Photostat copy of the certified copy of the deed of lease. In the said deed of lease, there is no mention at all that the income of the Housing Estate would be utilized to materialize the purposes of the Trust. In the deed of lease there is no recital that the possession had been handed over to the lessee OJ Trust though the possession was allegedly delivered to the lessee on 20-2-1980. The deed of lease was executed within a period of two months and sixteen days of the creation of Trust Deed.&nbsp; 

 19. Annexure-A to the affidavit-in-opposition of the respondent No. 3 is a letter dated 7-5-1980 addressed to the Ministry of Land Administration and Land Reforms by the Deputy Commissioner, Sylhet. In the said letter, the aforesaid Enquiry Report of the Sub-Divisional Officer had been referred to. The Deputy Commissioner opined that the land in question was in possession of the Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm. In the said letter the Deputy Commissioner stated that he for the first time came to know of the illegal settlement matter in favour of OJ Trust from the Superintendent of the Farm when he approached him and intimated of the alleged settlement. Thereafter, the respondent No. 3&#39;&#39;s Ministry made a series of letters and correspondences with the respondent No. 1&#39;&#39;s Ministry for cancelling the lease granted in favour of OJ Trust. It appears to us that in the backdrop of the letters and correspondences and the actual state of affairs, the Memo dated 21-5-1986 had been issued. Annexure-C to the writ petition is the Memo dated 21-5-1986 by which the respondent No. 1&#39;&#39;s Ministry cancelled the allotment in favour of petitioner OJ Trust with the decision that the Dairy Farm was a very important Government Project and no land of the Farm should be given to an individual on the plea that the same was recorded in collector&#39;&#39;s khatian as khas land and the Government Dairy Farm could not be penalised for wrong recording of the land as khas land. The Ministry in the said Memo asked the Deputy Commissioner to take appropriate step to revoke the lease deeds and to find out land elsewhere for settlement to OJ Trust and send proposal to the Ministry for consideration with copy to the petitioner. But neither the Deputy Commissioner nor the petitioner appears to have taken any further step in the matter.&nbsp; 

 20. Annexure-1 to the Affidavit-in-opposition filed on behalf of respondent No. 1 is Memo dated 23-8-84 from the Board of Land Administration to the Deputy Commissioner Sylhet stating that the land as sought for by OJ Trust belonged to the Government Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm, Sylhet and the land was wrongly leased out to the said Trust. In the said Memo, the Deputy Commissioner was advised to ask the applicant, OJ Trust, if it wanted alternative land, it might come up with such proposal and also directed the Deputy Commissioner to take the staff responsible in the matter to task. It appears from the Government file that proceeding has been started against the officer responsible in the matter, Mr. Abul Hashem, the then Circle Officer (Rev.) Sadar, Sylhet. 

 From the Government file we find two letters, one sent by the Secretary, Ministry of Fisheries and Livestock addressed to the Secretary, Ministry of Land Administration and Land Reforms requesting the said Secretary to cancel the illegal order of settlement with respect to land in question in favour of OJ Trust on recalling the order dated 24-12-88 by which the order of cancellation of the lease had been withdrawn and another letter from the Hon&#39;&#39;ble Minister, Ministry of Fisheries and Livestock addressed to the Hon&#39;&#39;ble Minister, Ministry of Land Administrative and Land Reforms requesting him to pass necessary orders to cancel the illegal lease granted in favour of petitioner OJ Trust. 

 21. From plethora of letters and correspondences annexed as annexures and also letters and correspondences from the Government file, it becomes evidently clear that the respondent No. 3&#39;&#39;s Ministry had been pursuing the respondent No. 1&#39;&#39;s Ministry for cancelling the illegal lease granted to OJ Trust. From all these annexure and letters, we find that the entire acquired 228 acres of land including the land in question since the year 1932 has been in possession of Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm and the land was illegally leased out to OJ Trust on the basis of the Enquiry Report submitted by the Circle Officer (Revenue), Kotwali, Syihet falsely showing the land in question as Government khas land by suppressing the fact that the same was in possession of the Dairy Farm. Thus, lease in favour of the petitioner OJ Trust was the result of fraudulent misrepresentation of facts to the respondent No. 1&#39;&#39;s Ministry.&nbsp; 

 22. It appears from the letter dated 20-2-80 (Annexure-E), written by the Government Tahsildar to the Circle Officer (Rev.) that he delivered possession of the land in question to the petitioner who accepted the same. But there is nothing in that letter how possession was delivered to the petitioner. On the other hand, it appears from the admission of the Deputy Secretary of the Ministry of LA &amp; LR made in his letter dated 16-1-85 (Annexure-G) that possession could not be delivered to the petitioner as the land was within the boundary of the Government Cattle Farm. Though in the office memorandum dated 5-1-86 (Annexure-H) relied on by the petitioner and seriously challenged by the respondents as fortuitous, the Ministry of Fisheries and Livestock agreed to surrender and hand over possession of the land in question to the petitioner, there is nothing to show that possession was, in fact, made over to the petitioner by the respondent No. 3 in pursuance of assurance made in the said office memorandum. On the other hand, petitioner&#39;&#39;s assertion in para 15 of the writ petition that an officer of the Ministry of Fisheries and Livestock went to the said Farm and told the people of that Farm that the land in question would be possessed and enjoyed by OJ Trust has been stoutly denied in para 12 of the Affidavit-in-opposition of the respondent No. 3 as nothing but downright falsehood. Even in that assertion, there is no name or designation of the Officer or the date and time when he went there to say so to make the assertion credible. In view of the above, we find that there is nothing to show that petitioner got actual delivery of possession in the land in question. Petitioner&#39;&#39;s assertion of making constructions has also been denied by the respondent No. 3. Even, there is no such assertion in petitioner&#39;&#39;s representation dated 4-12-88 (Annexure-J) for withdrawal of the order of cancellation of the allotment. Thus, we have no option but to hold that the delivery of possession of the land in question to the OJ Trust by the Tahsildar on 20-2-80 is a mere paper transaction and never an actual delivery of possession.&nbsp; 

 23. From the facts and circumstances of the case as noticed and discussed above, we find that lease in favour of the petitioner was the result of fraudulent misrepresentation of facts and respondent No. 1 though offered alternative land, the petitioner did not care to take advantage of that offer and at his insistence, the order of cancellation was withdrawn and ultimately the said order of withdrawal has been kept in abeyance by the impugned Telex Message. We have also noticed from the materials on record that the petitioner had easy access to the then concerned Minister and taking advantage of the same he exerted undue influence on the officers of the concerned Ministry and was able to manipulate things in his favour by obtaining false report within a very short time. We find that the whole transaction was vitiated by fraudulent misrepresentation. No one can take advantage of fraud relying on technicalities of law. Thus, we find no merit in the contention of the learned Advocate for the petitioner that OJ Trust acquired indefeasible title in the land in question by virtue of the lease deed and the impugned Telex Message is without lawful authority. Merely because the lease deed in question has not been revoked it cannot be said that the petitioner acquired title in the land in question by obtaining the lease deed from respondent No. 4 on fraudulent misrepresentation of facts by unduly influencing the concerned Government staff under order of the Ministry of Land which had no control and management of the land in question. Had there been no order of stay from this Court the Deputy Commissioner would have taken steps to revoke the lease deed as directed in the letter dated 21-5-86.&nbsp; 

 &quot;Government of the People&#39;&#39;s Republic of Bangladesh Ministry of Fisheries and Livestock&nbsp; 

 The undersigned is directed to say that the Government in this Ministry have been pleased to agree to surrender the 31 acres of land currently in possession of the Dairy Farm, Sylhet for handing over the same to the possession of one Mr. OR Jaigirdar as per the request of the Ministry of LA &amp; LR made under its Memo No.Vlll-267/84/53 dated 16-1-85.&nbsp; 

 26. The contents of the Office Memorandum is that Government in the Ministry of Fisheries and Livestock has been pleased to agree to surrender 31 acres of land currently in possession of the Sylhet Cattle Breeding-cum-Dairy Farm for handing over the possession in favour of Mr. Obaid Zaigirdar as per the request of the Ministry of LA and LR dated 16-1-85. It is the contention of the learned Deputy Attorney-General that there is no legal existence of any such Office Memorandum and the said Office Memorandum might have been obtained by illegally gaining over some corrupt Officers of the said Ministry. It appears from the government file that a letter dated 5-10-89 was sent to the Secretary, Ministry of Land Administration and Land Reforms by the Secretary, Ministry of Fisheries and Livestock. Paragraph-3 of the letter is very significant and the same is extracted below:&nbsp; 

 28. On the other hand, even in his representation dated 4-12-88, petitioner stated that Government Tahsildar handed over possession on 20-2-80. There is no assertion in that representation that in pursuance of office memorandum dated 5-1-86, Ministry of Fisheries and Livestock handed over possession to OJ Trust and the said trust made improvement or construction in the said land. Thus, it is clear that the assurance made in the said office memorandum was not acted upon. On the other hand, the allotment in favour of OJ Trust was cancelled on 21-5-86 about 4 months after. In view of the discussions made above and also the facts and circumstances of the case, we are of this view that the principle of estoppel is not at all attracted here. Thus, we do not find any substance in the second contention of Mr. Mahmudul Islam.&nbsp; 

 31. The facts and circumstances of the cited case is quite distinguishable and got no manner of application in the fact and circumstances of the present case. In our view, the said decision cannot be of any assistance to the petitioner who is not an innocent party in the transaction. Petitioner can take advantage of respondent No. 1&#39;&#39;s offer of lease of Government khas land elsewhere but he cannot insist on asserting title on the land of respondent No. 3&nbsp;
